`graph` took no argument but `--root`, so on a 105-task plan the fourth pilot got
285 lines and 12.6 KB of Mermaid: "a graph that size does not render usefully
anywhere, so on a plan of this shape the command is effectively unavailable"
(`docs/pilots/p00020.md` item 13). That finding dispositioned itself as not worth
fixing alone, and ADR-0017 changed it: an order that is *derived* has to be
inspectable, or it is an assertion the reader takes on trust.

`tree` is that inspection, and it answers in both directions. Upwards is why a
task is not offered yet; downwards is what finishing it releases, which is the
first input to `ready`'s rank (`A1-125`). So the two halves of the order's claim
are the two halves of this view.

```
$ aplan tree A-2
A-2  todo  What it made possible
  rank: releases 1, on the critical path, a chain of 2 behind it, 2d
  waits on: nothing
  releases:
    A-3  todo  And the one after that
      B-1  todo  In the next cut
        M-s1  todo  The second cut  (the cut it is in)
      M-s0  todo  The first cut  (the cut it is in)
    M-s0  (shown above)

```

A cut is labelled for what it is. ADR-0008 makes membership an edge, so every
task "releases" its own checkpoint, and `A1-125`'s rank excludes those on
purpose: unlabelled here, a reader checking the order against this view would
count `M-s0` and conclude the rank had forgotten it.

The rank is shown only for a task `ready` would offer, because it is an order
over offered work. A blocked task has no place in it.

```
$ aplan tree A-3
A-3  todo  And the one after that
  waits on:
    A-2  todo  What it made possible
  releases:
    B-1  todo  In the next cut
      M-s1  todo  The second cut  (the cut it is in)
    M-s0  todo  The first cut  (the cut it is in)

```

`--depth` bounds it, and a subtree cut short says how much of the plan it is not
showing: everything below the cut that this view did not print elsewhere, rather
than the children it stopped at. On a 4000-long chain those differ by 3998.

```
$ aplan tree A-2 --depth 1
A-2  todo  What it made possible
  rank: releases 1, on the critical path, a chain of 2 behind it, 2d
  waits on: nothing
  releases:
    A-3  todo  And the one after that
      ... 2 more tasks below, past --depth
    M-s0  todo  The first cut  (the cut it is in)

```

`graph` gains the two filters the pilot named, and they intersect: "the output
tasks in this cut" is one question, not two.

What it draws is the *dependency* graph. `ADR-0008` models a cut as a meta-task
that is `blocked_by` its members, which is a good decision for the model and
makes one field carry two facts: three quarters of this plan's edges were a cut
listing a member, and drawing them as constraint made the whole graph nine
layers deep and 177 nodes wide. Grouping is what `aplan roadmap` prints as a
heading, so `graph` leaves it out and says how much it left (`A1-216`).

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0
graph TD
    A-2["A-2: What it made possible"]
    A-3["A-3: And the one after that"]
    A-2 --> A-3
%% scoped to cut `s0`.
%% 3 cut-membership edge(s) and 2 task(s) with no dependency are not drawn: a cut listing its members is grouping, which `aplan roadmap` prints as a heading. `--membership` draws them.
%% 1 edge to a task outside this view is not drawn. `aplan tree <id>` follows them.

```

An edge is drawn only when both ends are in view, and what left is counted in
Mermaid's own comment syntax, so what a reader pastes anywhere is still a
diagram. A dangling arrow to a node nobody can see would say "something outside
blocks this" without saying what.

`--membership` draws the grouping too, dotted, so the two cannot be read as the
same relationship.

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0 --membership
graph TD
    A-1(["A-1: The groundwork"])
    A-2["A-2: What it made possible"]
    A-3["A-3: And the one after that"]
    M-s0["M-s0: The first cut"]
    A-2 --> A-3
    A-1 -.-> M-s0
    A-2 -.-> M-s0
    A-3 -.-> M-s0
%% scoped to cut `s0`.
%% 1 edge to a task outside this view is not drawn. `aplan tree <id>` follows them.

```

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0 --tag output
graph TD
    A-3["A-3: And the one after that"]
%% scoped to cut `s0`, and tag `output`.
%% 1 task in view and no edges between them: nothing here waits on anything else here.
%% 3 edges to tasks outside this view are not drawn. `aplan tree <id>` follows them.

```

Boxes and no arrows is itself a claim, so a view with no edges says which it is.

The scope and the rendering are independent decisions (`A1-193`). The same view
in DOT, which is what every other layout and analysis tool reads, and in JSON,
which is the graph as data rather than as any rendering of it. Both carry the
same nodes, the same edges and the same count of what left the view.

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0 --format dot
digraph plan {
    rankdir=TB;
    "A-2" [label="A-2: What it made possible", shape=box];
    "A-3" [label="A-3: And the one after that", shape=box];
    "A-2" -> "A-3";
    // scoped to cut `s0`.
    // 3 cut-membership edge(s) and 2 task(s) with no dependency are not drawn: a cut listing its members is grouping, which `aplan roadmap` prints as a heading. `--membership` draws them.
    // 1 edge to a task outside this view is not drawn. `aplan tree <id>` follows them.
}

```

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0 --tag output --format json
{
  "edges": [],
  "grouping": {
    "edges": 0,
    "tasks": 0
  },
  "hidden_edges": 3,
  "nodes": [
    {
      "done": false,
      "id": "A-3",
      "status": "todo",
      "title": "And the one after that"
    }
  ],
  "schema": "aplan/graph/v1",
  "scope": [
    "cut `s0`",
    "tag `output`"
  ]
}

```

And the same view through the explicit compatibility layout (`A1-219`). One row per task,
one lane per edge in flight, and edges merging where they arrive: fan-in is the
question a reader brings to a dependency graph. A filled node is done, a
hollow one is not, and the exact status remains beside the id. The fixed role
column is blank until `--focus` or `--critical` gives it something to say. Box
drawing is not guaranteed in a CI log, so a pipe gets the fallback table -- the
same drawing, different characters -- followed by its compact legend.

What it could not draw it says, in the sentence beneath: a scoped view that
quietly loses the edges leaving it answers a question the reader did not ask,
and every shape owes that answer, not only the two with a comment syntax.

```
$ aplan graph --cut s0 --format text --layout lanes
      o A-2  [todo] What it made possible
      o A-3  [todo] And the one after that

scoped to cut `s0`.

3 cut-membership edge(s) and 2 task(s) with no dependency are not drawn: a cut listing its
members is grouping, which `aplan roadmap` prints as a heading. `--membership` draws them.

1 edge to a task outside this view is not drawn. `aplan tree <id>` follows them.

legend: [F] focus; [B] direct blocker; [R] direct dependent; [C] critical route; `>` an exact critical step enters this node; letters combine.
status: `[status]` is exact; `*` dependency satisfied; `o` dependency unsatisfied; `=`/`!` critical edge.

```

`mermaid` is still what `graph` prints with no flag, so the shape a reader
already depends on did not move under them.

A filter that matches nothing is refused rather than answered with an empty
diagram, which renders as a blank rectangle and reads as "this plan has nothing
in it".

```
$ aplan graph --tag nope
? 2
aplan: nothing matches tag `nope`: no task is in view, so there is no graph

```

```
$ aplan graph --cut v9.9
? 2
aplan: no cut `v9.9` in this plan. It has: M-s0 (`s0`), M-s1 (`s1`)

```

```
$ aplan tree NOPE
? 2
aplan: no task `NOPE` in this plan

```
